            <ArticleTitle>Update on the pharmacologic therapy for chronic obstructive pulmonary disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>589-607, vi-vii</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is a treatable disease characterized by progressive airflow limitation. Prevention of disease progression; improvement of symptoms, exercise tolerance, and health status; and decrease in exacerbations and mortality are the goals of management. Inhaled short-acting bronchodilators are recommended for symptoms in mild disease, whereas inhaled long-acting bronchodilators are recommended for maintenance therapy of daily symptoms. When symptoms are not controlled using one bronchodilator, combining bronchodilators may be more effective. Combining a long-acting beta-agonist with an inhaled corticosteroid is more effective than either agent alone. Several novel therapies are in different stages of development.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
